.Dd November 19, 2026
.Dt SCHED_SETSCHEDULER 2
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm sched_setscheduler ,
.Nm sched_getscheduler ,
.Nm sched_setparam ,
.Nm sched_getparam ,
.Nm sched_setattr ,
.Nm sched_getattr ,
.Nm sched_get_priority_max ,
.Nm sched_get_priority_min ,
.Nm sched_rr_get_interval ,
.Nm sched_setaffinity ,
.Nm sched_getaffinity ,
.Nm sched_yield ,
.Nm getcpu
.Nd scheduling policy, priority, affinity and yielding
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In sched.h
.Ft int
.Fn sched_setscheduler "pid_t pid" "int policy" "const struct sched_param *param"
.Ft int
.Fn sched_getscheduler "pid_t pid"
.Ft int
.Fn sched_setparam "pid_t pid" "const struct sched_param *param"
.Ft int
.Fn sched_getparam "pid_t pid" "struct sched_param *param"
.Ft int
.Fn sched_setattr "pid_t pid" "struct sched_attr *attr" "unsigned int flags"
.Ft int
.Fn sched_getattr "pid_t pid" "struct sched_attr *attr" "unsigned int size" "unsigned int flags"
.Ft int
.Fn sched_get_priority_max "int policy"
.Ft int
.Fn sched_get_priority_min "int policy"
.Ft int
.Fn sched_rr_get_interval "pid_t pid" "struct timespec *tp"
.Ft int
.Fn sched_setaffinity "pid_t pid" "size_t cpusetsize" "const cpu_set_t *mask"
.Ft int
.Fn sched_getaffinity "pid_t pid" "size_t cpusetsize" "cpu_set_t *mask"
.Ft int
.Fn sched_yield "void"
.Ft int
.Fn getcpu "unsigned int *cpu" "unsigned int *node"
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Xr sched 7
describes the policies, how the scheduler chooses a thread, and the fair
server that reserves time for ordinary threads.
.Pp
A
.Fa pid
of 0 names the caller; any other names a process that has not been
reaped.
A change to
.Dv SCHED_FIFO ,
.Dv SCHED_RR
or
.Dv SCHED_DEADLINE
sets the thread's timer slack to 0, so that its sleeps end at their time,
and a change from one of them to another policy gives the thread its
default slack back, as on Linux; see
.Dv PR_SET_TIMERSLACK
in
.Xr prctl 2 .
.Pp
.Fn sched_getscheduler
returns the thread's policy,
.Dv SCHED_OTHER
.Pq 0 ,
.Dv SCHED_BATCH
.Pq 3
or
.Dv SCHED_IDLE
.Pq 5 ,
.Dv SCHED_FIFO
.Pq 1
or
.Dv SCHED_RR
.Pq 2 ,
or
.Dv SCHED_DEADLINE
.Pq 6 ,
with
.Dv SCHED_RESET_ON_FORK
added when it was set with it, and
.Fn sched_getparam
stores its base priority, without a PI donation.
.Fn sched_setscheduler
sets an ordinary policy with priority 0, with
.Dv SCHED_RESET_ON_FORK
or without, for a caller that may change the thread: one with effective
user ID 0, or whose effective user ID equals the thread's real or
effective user ID.
.Fn sched_setparam
keeps the policy and changes its base priority.
An unprivileged caller may leave
.Dv SCHED_IDLE
only when the thread's
.Dv RLIMIT_NICE ,
0 by default, permits its stored nice value.
Clearing
.Dv SCHED_RESET_ON_FORK
also requires privilege.
Both checks can fail with
.Er EPERM .
A fork's child has its parent's policy and parameters without
.Dv SCHED_RESET_ON_FORK ,
which Linux clears in the child, and an exec keeps both.
With the flag, a real-time or deadline policy becomes
.Dv SCHED_OTHER
at nice 0 in the child, a negative nice value becomes 0, and a slice
asked for the default.
A
.Dv SCHED_DEADLINE
thread without the flag may not fork, and
.Xr fork 2
and
.Xr clone 2
fail with
.Er EAGAIN .
A privileged caller can set
.Dv SCHED_FIFO
or
.Dv SCHED_RR
with priority 1 to 99.
An unprivileged caller may enter a real-time policy only while the
thread's
.Dv RLIMIT_RTPRIO
is above 0, and raise the priority only up to it; it may always lower
the priority of its present real-time policy.
The limit is 0 by default, as on Linux, which allows neither.
.Dv SCHED_DEADLINE
takes its parameters from
.Fn sched_setattr
alone, and
.Fn sched_setscheduler
and
.Fn sched_setparam
refuse it with
.Er EINVAL ,
as they do
.Dv SCHED_EXT
and the numbers Linux does not define.
.Pp
.Fn sched_setattr
sets the policy and parameters a
.Vt struct sched_attr
gives:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
struct sched_attr {
	uint32_t size;		/* 48, 56, or 0 for 48 */
	uint32_t sched_policy;
	uint64_t sched_flags;	/* SCHED_FLAG_ */
	int32_t sched_nice;	/* ordinary policies */
	uint32_t sched_priority;	/* SCHED_FIFO, SCHED_RR */
	uint64_t sched_runtime;	/* ns */
	uint64_t sched_deadline;	/* ns, SCHED_DEADLINE */
	uint64_t sched_period;	/* ns, SCHED_DEADLINE */
	uint32_t sched_util_min;	/* size 56 */
	uint32_t sched_util_max;
};
.Ed
.Pp
with the same layout on i386 and x86-64.
A
.Fa size
below 48 or past 4096 fails with
.Er E2BIG ,
and so does a larger one than 56 whose further bytes are not all 0; either
writes 56 into the caller's
.Fa size .
It takes each policy
.Fn sched_setscheduler
takes, with the same checks and result, and
.Dv SCHED_DEADLINE .
For
.Dv SCHED_OTHER
and
.Dv SCHED_BATCH
it sets the nice value, clamped to -20 to 19,
and a slice of
.Fa sched_runtime
nanoseconds, clamped to 0.1 to 100 ms, or the default for 0.
The fair scheduler uses the slice to set the distance to the thread's
next virtual deadline, scaled by its weight.
Lowering the nice value past what the thread's
.Dv RLIMIT_NICE
allows, 20 less its soft value, needs privilege, and fails with
.Er EPERM .
.Dv SCHED_IDLE
keeps the nice value and slice unchanged.
.Dv SCHED_FLAG_RESET_ON_FORK
(1) is
.Dv SCHED_RESET_ON_FORK ;
.Dv SCHED_FLAG_KEEP_POLICY
(8) keeps the thread's policy and its reset flag;
.Dv SCHED_FLAG_KEEP_PARAMS
(16) checks the thread's present parameters in place of the structure's
and changes only the reset flag;
.Dv SCHED_FLAG_RECLAIM
(2) and
.Dv SCHED_FLAG_DL_OVERRUN
(4) are kept with a deadline thread's parameters.
The utilization clamps,
.Dv SCHED_FLAG_UTIL_CLAMP_MIN
(32) and
.Dv SCHED_FLAG_UTIL_CLAMP_MAX
(64), fail with
.Er EOPNOTSUPP ,
as from a Linux built without them, and with
.Er EINVAL
in a structure of 48 bytes.
.Pp
.Dv SCHED_DEADLINE
needs privilege.
Its
.Fa sched_runtime
must be 1024 ns at least and at most the deadline, the deadline nonzero
and at most the period, and the period, or the deadline when the period
is 0, from 100 us to 2^22 us, or the call fails with
.Er EINVAL .
A thread whose affinity leaves out a processor that is started fails with
.Er EPERM .
The scheduler cannot provide deadline bandwidth, so every request that
passes these checks fails with
.Er EBUSY ,
as Linux's sched_dl_overflow refuses unavailable bandwidth.
Such a thread's affinity may not then leave out a processor:
.Fn sched_setaffinity
fails with
.Er EBUSY .
.Pp
.Fn sched_getattr
writes the thread's policy, its
.Dv SCHED_FLAG_RESET_ON_FORK ,
and the parameters of its policy: a deadline thread's runtime, deadline,
period and flags, a real-time thread's priority, or an ordinary thread's
nice value and slice in nanoseconds.
An ordinary thread's slice is the one it asked for, or the base slice
it last took, which
.Xr sched 7
describes, as on Linux.
It writes as many bytes as
.Fa size
holds, at most 56, and that count in the structure's
.Fa size .
A
.Fa size
below 48 or past 4096, or
.Fa flags
other than 0, fails with
.Er EINVAL .
.Pp
.Fn sched_get_priority_max
and
.Fn sched_get_priority_min
give Linux's range for each policy Linux has, whether or not it runs here:
1 to 99 for
.Dv SCHED_FIFO
and
.Dv SCHED_RR ,
and 0 to 0 for
.Dv SCHED_OTHER ,
.Dv SCHED_BATCH ,
.Dv SCHED_IDLE ,
.Dv SCHED_DEADLINE
and
.Dv SCHED_EXT .
.Pp
.Fn sched_rr_get_interval
stores the selected thread's interval in
.Fa tp .
For
.Dv SCHED_RR
it is the configured full slice, 100 ms by default.
A positive value written to
.Pa /proc/sys/kernel/sched_rr_timeslice_ms
is rounded up to a whole tick of 4 ms for this call and for scheduling.
For an ordinary thread, the call reads the thread's slice rounded down to
a whole tick if its processor's root fair queue has load, or zero if the
queue has no load.
An ordinary slice shorter than 4 ms therefore reads as zero.
The interval is zero for
.Dv SCHED_FIFO
or
.Dv SCHED_DEADLINE
as on Linux.
.Pp
.Fn sched_getaffinity
stores the processors the process may run on, of those started, a bit
for each, and returns the bytes it stored: a long for every 32 or 64
processors up to the highest started, as Linux sizes its mask.
.Fn sched_setaffinity
reads at most that many bytes of the mask, binds the process to the
started processors the mask has, whatever it says of others, and moves a
process that runs on a processor it no longer has before the call returns.
For another running thread, the caller waits until it switches away;
a later affinity request that permits its current processor also ends the wait.
A fork passes the set on, and an exec keeps it.
.Pp
.Fn getcpu
stores the number of the processor the caller runs on, which may change as
soon as the call returns, and 0 for the NUMA node, where its non-null
arguments point.
On x86-64, musl and glibc call it through the vDSO, which reads the number
with
.Ic rdtscp
and enters the kernel only on a processor without that instruction
.Pq Xr vdso 7 .
.Pp
.Fn sched_yield
puts a real-time caller behind the ready threads of its priority.
An eligible ordinary caller gives up the rest of its slice by moving its
virtual runtime to its deadline and taking a new deadline.
The scheduler then chooses a thread.
The call can return at once when no other thread is ready.
.Ss Differences from Linux
Linux runs a
.Dv SCHED_DEADLINE
thread before every real-time thread, by the earliest deadline, and stops
it once it has used its runtime in a period; this scheduler has no
deadline class, and refuses that policy with
.Er EBUSY
after checking its arguments, privilege and affinity.
.Ss System call interface
The i386 system call numbers are 154 for
.Fn sched_setparam ,
155 for
.Fn sched_getparam ,
156 for
.Fn sched_setscheduler ,
157 for
.Fn sched_getscheduler ,
158 for
.Fn sched_yield ,
159 for
.Fn sched_get_priority_max ,
160 for
.Fn sched_get_priority_min ,
161 for
.Fn sched_rr_get_interval ,
241 for
.Fn sched_setaffinity ,
and 242 for
.Fn sched_getaffinity ;
on x86-64 they are 142 to 148 in the same order but for
.Fn sched_yield ,
which is 24, and 203 and 204.
.Fn sched_setattr
and
.Fn sched_getattr
are 351 and 352 on i386 and 314 and 315 on x86-64, and an i386 program on
the x86-64 kernel has them at 351 and 352.
The raw
.Fn sched_getaffinity
returns the bytes of the mask it wrote, a long for each long's bits of
processors up to the highest started: four on i386 with 32 processors or
fewer, and eight with more and on x86-64.
It fails with
.Er EINVAL
for a
.Fa cpusetsize
shorter than that or not a multiple of a long; the C library clears the
rest of a larger set and returns 0.
The raw
.Fn sched_setaffinity
reads at most that many bytes of the mask.
On i686,
.Fn sched_rr_get_interval
writes a timespec with two 32-bit fields.
An i386 program on the x86-64 kernel, which
.Xr ia32 7
describes, has the i386 calls: its mask is of 32-bit longs, four bytes
with 32 processors or fewer, as on i386,
.Nm sched_rr_get_interval
writes the same timespec, and
.Nm sched_rr_get_interval_time64
(423) a timespec of 64-bit fields.
Linux also provides call 423 on the native 32-bit kernel.
.Pp
musl implements
.Fn sched_setscheduler ,
.Fn sched_getscheduler ,
.Fn sched_setparam
and
.Fn sched_getparam
as failures with
.Er ENOSYS
and does not call the kernel, since Linux's calls act on a thread and
POSIX's on a process; glibc calls it.
.Sh RETURN VALUES
.Fn sched_getscheduler
returns the policy,
.Fn sched_get_priority_max
and
.Fn sched_get_priority_min
the priority, and the other functions 0.
On failure they return -1 and set
.Va errno .
The kernel returns the negative error number.
.Sh EXAMPLES
The default suite's
.Pa tests/musl/schedmusl.c
yields, reads the policy, the parameter, the priority ranges, the time
slice and the affinity, which is every processor
.Pa /proc/cpuinfo
lists, sets
.Dv SCHED_OTHER ,
.Dv SCHED_BATCH
and
.Dv SCHED_IDLE
and reads each back, sets an affinity of the last processor alone, after which
.Fn getcpu
returns that processor, and checks the refusals of the other policies, of
an affinity of a processor past the last, and of another user's process.
.Pp
.Pa tests/schedattrprobe.c
sets each policy with
.Fn sched_setattr
and reads it back with
.Fn sched_getattr ,
.Fn sched_getscheduler
and
.Fn sched_getparam ,
and checks the structure's size rules, the flags, the slices, the
deadline refusal and affinity checks, what a fork's child takes, the
round-robin interval, the refusals to a caller without root and what
.Dv RLIMIT_NICE
and
.Dv RLIMIT_RTPRIO
allow it.
.Sh ERRORS
.Bl -tag -width "[EOPNOTSUPP]"
.It Bq Er E2BIG
A
.Vt struct sched_attr
is of a size below 48 or past 4096, or larger than 56 with a byte past 56
that is not 0.
.It Bq Er EBUSY
The scheduler cannot provide deadline bandwidth, or a deadline thread's
affinity would leave out a processor.
.It Bq Er EFAULT
A parameter, structure, time slice or mask cannot be read or written.
.It Bq Er EINVAL
A
.Fa pid
is negative, a
.Fa param
is null, a policy or priority is one this kernel does not run, a policy
number is unknown to
.Fn sched_get_priority_max
or
.Fn sched_get_priority_min ,
a
.Fa cpusetsize
for
.Fn sched_getaffinity
is shorter than the mask or not a multiple of a long, or a mask for
.Fn sched_setaffinity
has no processor that is started; or for
.Fn sched_setattr
and
.Fn sched_getattr ,
the structure is null, the flags argument is not 0, a flag is unknown,
the deadline parameters are out of their bounds, or the clamps are given
in 48 bytes, and for
.Fn sched_getattr
the size is below 48 or past 4096.
.It Bq Er EOPNOTSUPP
.Fn sched_setattr
was given the utilization clamps.
.It Bq Er EPERM
The caller may not change the process, asked without privilege to enter
a real-time policy or raise its priority past what
.Dv RLIMIT_RTPRIO
allows, or asked without privilege to leave
.Dv SCHED_IDLE
or lower the nice value past what
.Dv RLIMIT_NICE
allows, to clear
.Dv SCHED_RESET_ON_FORK
or to set
.Dv SCHED_DEADLINE ;
or a thread whose affinity leaves out a processor was given
.Dv SCHED_DEADLINE .
.It Bq Er ESRCH
No process has the ID
.Fa pid .
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr fork 2 ,
.Xr getpriority 2 ,
.Xr prctl 2 ,
.Xr proc 5 ,
.Xr sched 7 ,
.Xr vdso 7
